1. Introduction
1.1 Purpose
This Software Requirements Specification defines the functional and non-functional requirements for the web-based Project Management System. It is the reference for implementation, testing, acceptance and change control.
1.2 Scope
The PMS supports user registration and login, profile management, project creation and membership, task creation and assignment, task status/priority/deadline management, and project progress monitoring.
1.3 Audience
Developers, QA/Test Lead, Test Engineer, Project Manager, project users and course evaluators.
1.4 Definitions
PMS – Project Management System; UI – User Interface; DB – Database; API – Application Programming Interface; RBAC – Role-Based Access Control; RTM – Requirements Traceability Matrix; SRS – Software Requirements Specification; SAD – Software Architecture and Design Specification; STP – Software Test Plan.
1.5 Revision and baseline note
Version 1.1 preserves the v1.0 requirement identifiers and expands their acceptance criteria and traceability. It also records a proposed sprint recovery schedule. The schedule change requires team/instructor confirmation; historical dates must not be backdated.
2. Overall description
2.1 Product perspective
The PMS is a browser-based web application with a presentation layer, server-side application/API logic and PostgreSQL data storage. The documented technology baseline is Next.js, Node.js and PostgreSQL. The exact repository implementation must be checked before sign-off.
2.2 Major functions
•	Register users and authenticate login/logout.
•	Manage the authenticated user's own profile.
•	Create, view and update project details according to permissions.
•	Add members to projects and restrict project visibility by membership/authorization.
•	Create tasks, assign tasks to valid project users and view assigned tasks.
•	Update task status and manage priority/deadline information.
•	Calculate and display project progress based on completed tasks.
•	Enforce authentication, authorization, input validation and secure communication.
2.3 User roles and permissions
Role	Intended responsibility
Administrator	Create projects, manage project details and add project members.
Project Manager	Create tasks and set/update task priority and deadlines within authorized projects.
Task Manager	Assign tasks to valid project users and manage permitted assignment operations.
Project member / authenticated user	View only authorized projects/tasks, view assigned tasks, update permitted task status and edit own profile.

Role clarification: the original SRS names Administrator, Project Manager and Task Manager; the original SAD also describes a basic User role. This revision uses “project member/authenticated user” for basic participant behavior. The team must confirm this mapping and keep it consistent across implementation, Jira, SAD and tests.
2.4 Operating environment and constraints
•	Client: modern web browser on Windows, Linux or macOS.
•	Application/runtime: Next.js and Node.js as the documented baseline.
•	Database: PostgreSQL.
•	HTTP may be used in local development as configured; deployed traffic carrying credentials/protected data must use HTTPS.
•	Project/task information must not be exposed to users without authorization.
•	Use dummy/test data in screenshots and test evidence; do not commit credentials or secrets.
3. External interface requirements
3.1 User interface
Expected interfaces include login/registration, dashboard, project list/details, task list/details, profile functions and project progress information. Screens should provide understandable validation and error feedback and reflect user permissions.
3.2 Software interfaces
The documented baseline is a Next.js application using server-side route handlers/business logic and PostgreSQL. Exact endpoint paths, payloads and response schemas must match the implementation and be documented in the SAD.
3.3 Communication
Browser/server requests use the configured client-server channel. Production/deployed traffic carrying credentials or protected data must use HTTPS.
4. Functional requirements
Req ID	Requirement	Priority	Acceptance criteria
PMS-F-001	Registration and login	High	A user can register with valid required details and log in with correct credentials. Invalid credentials are rejected safely; protected functionality requires authentication.
PMS-F-002	Profile management	High	An authenticated user can view/update permitted own-profile fields. A user cannot modify another user's profile without separately authorized admin capability.
PMS-F-003	Administrator creates projects	Medium	An authorized administrator can create a project with valid required details; it is persisted and visible to authorized members.
PMS-F-004	View projects	High	A user sees only projects they belong to or are otherwise explicitly authorized to access.
PMS-F-005	Project manager creates tasks	High	An authorized project manager can create a task associated with a project they manage.
PMS-F-006	Task manager assigns tasks	High	An authorized task manager can assign a task to a valid user permitted to participate in the project.
PMS-F-007	View assigned tasks	High	A user can view tasks assigned to them.
PMS-F-008	Update task status	High	An authorized assigned user can update task status; the change persists after reload.
PMS-F-009	Task priority and deadline	Medium	A task stores/displays priority and deadline; invalid values are rejected or handled according to documented validation rules.
PMS-F-010	Display project progress	Medium	The system displays project progress based on task completion using the agreed calculation rule.
PMS-F-011	Manage project details	Medium	An authorized administrator can view/update valid project details; unauthorized changes are rejected.
PMS-F-012	Add project members	High	An authorized administrator can add a valid user to a project; membership persists and affects authorized visibility.
PMS-F-013	Project manager sets priority/deadline	Medium	An authorized project manager can set/update task priority and deadline; unauthorized roles cannot change fields they do not control.
PMS-F-014	View authorized task statuses	Medium	Users can view task statuses only for tasks/projects they are authorized to access.
PMS-F-015	Progress based on completed tasks	Medium	Project progress is recalculated from task completion using the agreed formula; identical data produces a consistent result.

5. Non-functional requirements
Req ID	Category	Priority	Acceptance criteria / measurement
PMS-NF-001	Performance	High	Normal user requests should respond within 3 seconds under normal test conditions. Record environment, sample size and observed times.
PMS-NF-002	Authorization/security	High	Users cannot access unauthorized projects/tasks. Verify direct URL/API access as well as UI visibility.
PMS-NF-003	Data consistency	High	Project/task changes persist correctly; invalid references and inconsistent updates are rejected.
PMS-NF-004	Concurrent use	Medium	Multiple users can use the system without data corruption or significant degradation. Agree a numeric concurrency target before formal load testing.
PMS-NF-005	Usability	Medium	Users can identify/use main project/task functions with clear labels, validation and error feedback.

5.1 Security requirements
Req ID	Security requirement	Priority	Acceptance criteria
PMS-SR-001	Project/task authorization	High	Access is restricted according to user authorization and project membership.
PMS-SR-002	Authentication before protected functions	High	Unauthenticated requests to protected functions are denied or redirected to login.
PMS-SR-003	Own-profile modification	High	A user may modify only their own profile unless a separately authorized admin capability is specified.
PMS-SR-004	Role-based access control	High	Administrator, Project Manager and Task Manager actions follow agreed role permissions; basic member permissions are consistently defined.
PMS-SR-005	Protected communication	High	Credentials/protected data use HTTPS in deployed environments; secrets are not logged or committed.

6. Quality attributes and acceptance
•	Registration/login works and invalid credentials are rejected.
•	Authorized project operations and project membership work correctly.
•	Authorized task creation, assignment, viewing and status updates work correctly.
•	Priority, deadlines and progress are stored/displayed consistently.
•	Unauthorized direct URL/API access is rejected.
•	Normal-request performance is measured against the 3-second target.
•	All high-priority requirements have linked tests and implementation/test status is reported honestly.
